# Ignore what the toolchain generates, not everything-except-a-list.
#
# The old rule was `*` plus an allow-list of .ady, .md and .txt. It did its
# job for the build output, and quietly dropped every new kind of source
# file nobody had thought to add to the list -- .awk, .sh, .html and .conf
# fixtures were written, committed with `git add -A`, pushed, and were not
# in the repository. `git add` says nothing when it skips an ignored file,
# so the first sign of it was a reader not finding the files.
#
# What is actually generated in here:
#
#   * ady2nim drops a symlink to the built binary next to each compiled
#     source: same name, extension removed. That is the one pattern worth
#     keeping, and "a name with no dot in it" is what it comes to.
#   * ady2py writes <stem>_gen.py next to the source it transpiled.
#   * a few examples take a lock while they run and leave the file behind.
#
# Everything else is a source file and belongs in the repository.

# Build output: the extensionless symlinks, by way of "has no dot at all".
*
!*/
!*.*

# ... and the generated files that do have an extension.
*_gen.py
*.locked

# Makefile has no dot in its name and is not build output.
!Makefile
